BLE-ACTIVATE-1 / BLE-GATT-1 host probe against the DFR1154 autonomous node
2026-09-07

PEER      DFR1154 (FireBeetle 2 ESP32-S3), scenario 4 (BLE advertise diagnostic)
FIRMWARE  919110FC97FF942A77422534BA4BED33386232EB9DCFC3500769E47C2893BA69
HOST      Windows, MediaTek Bluetooth adapter, python bleak (WinRT backend)
TOKEN     0xA5C30F17, the firmware's compiled-in diagnostic constant

--- scan -------------------------------------------------------------------

$ python ble_probe.py scan --token 0xA5C30F17 --seconds 12

  E8:F6:0A:86:7B:D9  rssi=-40
    service data      00000000A5C30F17  (8 bytes)
    length is 8       yes
    upper 4 bytes 0   yes
    endpoint_token    0xa5c30f17
    matches wanted    YES

1 MCL advertiser(s)

The Service Data structure for the MCL 128-bit UUID is present, is exactly
eight bytes, and carries the endpoint_token zero-extended and big-endian, which
is BLE-ACTIVATE-1 section 3 byte for byte. A stack that shares no code with the
firmware parsed it.

--- connect ----------------------------------------------------------------

$ python ble_probe.py connect --token 0xA5C30F17 --seconds 15 --wait 5

scanning 15.0s for a connectable MCL advertiser
found E8:F6:0A:86:7B:D9
connected: True
  MCL-RX present properties=['write-without-response', 'write']
  MCL-TX present properties=['notify']
writing 40 bytes as 3 fragment(s) at the minimum MTU
  reassembled 40 bytes: 0102030405060708090A0B0C0D0E0F101112131415161718191A1B1C1D1E1F202122232425262728
frames received back: 1

40 bytes needs three fragments at the 23-byte minimum MTU, so the START, middle
and END cases were all exercised, in both directions: the host fragmented and
the board reassembled, then the board fragmented and the host reassembled. The
bytes came back identical.

--- the board's own log ----------------------------------------------------

MCLAUTO phase QUIESCE free_heap=87840
MCLAUTO phase ACTIVATE free_heap=87840
MCLAUTO BLE up free_heap=16124 largest=8180
MCLAUTO BLE advertising token=A5C30F17 beacon=00000000A5C30F17
MCLAUTO ble diagnostic advertise up free_heap=12116 largest=7668
MCLAUTO diag frame in 40 bytes 01020304...
MCLAUTO diag echo rc=0

THE NUMBER THAT MATTERED. The board advertised, accepted a connection, received
three fragments, reassembled a frame and notified three fragments back with
12 116 bytes of free heap and a largest block of 7 668. The memory spike had
left this as the open question -- BLE fits, but "TIGHT" -- and a connection at
this operating point is now measured rather than hoped for.

--- what this does NOT establish -------------------------------------------

Independent interoperability. The probe was written by the same author, from
the same documents, as the firmware it probes. It shares no source, which makes
it useful for catching an encoder that disagrees with its own specification; it
cannot catch a specification both halves misread the same way.
